\name{cellucid_field_export}
\alias{cellucid_field_export}
\title{Export a field}
\usage{
cellucid_field_export(viewer, field)
}
\arguments{
\item{viewer}{A viewer \code{\link{cellucid_view}()} or \code{\link{cellucid_serve}()} returned.}
\item{field}{A column of the cells by name (source "obs"), categorical or continuous. A named list. Required. \describe{\item{\code{key}}{The column name as the app shows it. Text (at least 1 character). Required.}\item{\code{source}}{A column of the cells. Exactly \code{"obs"}. Required.}}}
}
\value{
A \code{cellucid_action_request}, at once and invisibly: the action is sent now, or when the viewer's channel opens.
\code{\link{cellucid_action_result}(request)} returns its outcome: a list of \code{bytes}, \code{field} (The field exported), \code{file} (The file written), \code{kind}.
}
\description{
Write a column of the cells (categorical or continuous) as an obs-column CSV, as its Export button in Color by does: every open cell and its value, which cellucid.pull() in Python and cellucid_pull() in R read back.
}
\details{
The viewer's action \code{field.export} (Exports). It acts outside the viewer's state and history (a file the person receives in the browser, a publication, or what the browser keeps for later visits), which Undo does not take back: it makes no step of the history.

When an agent (Claude, ChatGPT) runs it, the viewer asks the person first (writes-file); from R it is not asked.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{cellucid_act}()} sends any action by its type, and \code{\link{cellucid_action_result}()}
collects its answer.
}
\examples{
if (interactive() && requireNamespace("SeuratObject", quietly = TRUE)) {
  data("pbmc_small", package = "SeuratObject")
  viewer <- cellucid_view(SeuratObject::UpdateSeuratObject(pbmc_small))
  request <- cellucid_field_export(viewer, field = list(key = "cell_type",
      source = "obs"))
  print(request)
  # cellucid_action_result(request) waits for its outcome.
  cellucid_stop(viewer)
}
}
